With a head still sore from waking up in a pile of rocks, and still no memory of how she even ended up there, Zan made her way out of the mining post only to find her blood sisters dead from a treacherous force. Whoever those cultists were, they meant business, and if Demons were behind this, things were about to become much worse... She rushed through the secret tunnel towards her home town, grip tightened around that hefty axe, hoping that the enemy hadn't breached the defense. The sooner Zan could join the Iron Division warriors in crushing their invaders, the sooner they could make them pay for this insolent aggression. She emerges by Valc's plaza, yet no sign of ongoing battle can be heard...
Wiping the mixture of blood and sweat from her forehead, Zan shambled towards the giant Barracks gate. With grit teeth, she hardly refrained from smashing the switch open in anger. Treason! Treachery! How did the cultists time their invasion so well? How could their magics work past the crystal wards? How could they kill everyone so fast? Suddenly, her thoughts were shattered by a spike of pain coursing down her spine. In a split blackout, a word echoed through her mind... Abhaoth... A word? Or a name? She rubbed her temple, trying to grasp its meaning, but her memories were clouded still... All Zan knew right now was her duty towards protecting the Crystal Heart. There's no telling what Demons could do once they'd reach the very core of the Iron Division's technology... ...the Barracks gate begins to open. If the garrison still holds, then this is the best place where she can rally up with the defenders, and get revenge.
The Half-Orc hopped into the ventilation shaft, sweaty arms shaking in exhaustion. As she crawled forward, she could feel her battered body growing less responsive, beckoning for rest. The air seemed to be heating up... Visions of war rolled before her eyes. A dark citadel surrounded by hellish plains. Orcs were raging around her, clenched into battle with the demonic horde. She tried to move, but something held her in place. A dark figure with a gleaming pair of orange eyes. Abhaoth... The name suddenly rang her awake, head almost hitting against the narrow vent in surprise. How long had she been asleep? Moving forward, adrenaline coursed up her veins as the thought of the demon invasion brought her back on track. She had to reach the portal, and use it to get to the Crystal Heart before the aggressors did. But with an army of cultists around, it won't be easy.
As she waddled through a cloud of dust and rubble, Zan noticed an already familiar presence waiting at the end of the ruined corridor. She ran towards it, but much to her expectations, the dark creature vanished into thin air, leaving only a puff of black smoke behind. A series of curses echoed through the Plaza's caverns as Zan proceeded towards the park, her axe up and ready. How was she supposed to fight this dark thing after all? It always seemed to guide her towards more danger... Alas, the park greets her with the sound of restless insects and the dark chill of what may lie ahead...
Zan glanced back at the pile of dead cultists littering the portal cave. If it wasn't for her shielding amulet absorbing the energy surge, her body may have well been among theirs. She could only hope that the strange dark mass surrounding the portal would cease to grow now... The cultists must have thrown in all they had, but nothing could stop her now. Taking a deep breath, she walked into the waygate, an eerie force snatching her entire body into a twisted spiral, dragging her across unknown space. Suddenly, a red light flashed through the noisy wormhole. The Half-Orc winced and gasped, but before she could even wonder how the cultists' corruption might have affected the portal, she was blinded by a pulsating white glow... Much to her despair, the waygate spits her back into the Grove. She reaches around, but all her gear seems to have vanished. Something must have gone terribly wrong...
A loud shriek pierced and echoed through the abyss, as Nithriel conjured all her powers to preserve her life. A bright flash of light briefly blinded the horned warrior and she could do little but stand and watch the elven warlock ascend towards the starry nether. The battle was over, but it somehow felt like it had not even truly began. As the hellish sky faded into serenity, a dark portal emerged from the ground. Zan couldn't help but ponder if any of that was actually real. As soon as she entered the portal, a sudden shock woke her up. Tongues of crisp wind whistled through the trees spreading scents of pine. Where was she now? The mountain scape surrounding her looked chillingly familiar - she was outside! Farther away than ever from stopping Nithriel! With a tired sigh, she grasps her gun and steps forward, nervously wondering what happened to Abhaoth, and if she'll turn up to point the way before the Heart is lost.
A broad grin shaped up the warrior's face as the Ward's mechanism came alive. The ground began to rumble, a gleaming white crystal emerging from underneath. Unholy pentagrams began to fizzle and blow up - the few lucky cultists who had managed to teleport through in time were swiftly butchered by the Half-Orc. The rest who warped as the Ward unleashed itself simply died before they could even finish their interdimensional journey... Striking this lethal blow to the invaders' logistics would definitely change the tides of the assault. Zan couldn't help but wonder if Abhaoth was involved with her waking up so close to where this foul invasion seems to have begun. Nonetheless, she was nowhere to be seen now, and there was little time to be wasted. She marched on upwards, knowing of a dwarven fortress atop the mountain that was allied with the Iron Division. Those stout warriors would surely aid her to retake Valc. Half a day later, a thick, heavy mist and the cold breath of snow greet her.    She is getting closer to the dwarf fortress.
With a piercing metallic shrill, the lift plummeted into the abyss. It took no longer than seconds for a deafening thud to echo into Valc's deserted caves as the falling mass of iron came to a thundering halt. Groaning in pain, the half-orc warrior cupped her head, ears still ringing with the sound of the violent landing. A few dusty coughs were all that would delay her now. At last, she is back into the mountain's bowels. Close to the Crystal Heart. And even closer to the betrayer.
Zan thundered her way out of the clinic. The horrible experiments Lith had given life to only enraged her further.    If it hadn't been for Tiati, she would have become an abomination herself by now! As the large crystal gate opened, she wondered about Chieftain Zura who seemed to be still alive inside Lith's experiment tank...  she was most likely poisoned now. Perhaps it was better to leave her behind. But now the warrior had reached the Lancer Temple and all her thoughts would soon shift towards the final battle that seemed to draw ever closer... She is finally here. The Crystal Heart awaits ahead...
Zan burst through the entrance to the castle, her steps darting over the crimson carpet in a mere moment, heart pounding as she could feel Nithriel's presence at hand. Up front, a narrow stone corridor invited her to step through, with an already familiar silhouette awaiting her at the end... Abhaoth. The warrior grinned, knowing that Nithriel must have been losing her power if she couldn't suppress the shadowy guide any longer. But before she could finish savoring the moment, Zan's heartbeat would suddenly come to a halt. The shadow vanished. And as thick bars descended from the ceiling, so did the floor underneath her feet. A scream left her lungs as she fell beneath... Waking up in a pile of dark muck, the Half-Orc can hardly get her bearings before a Crawler greets her with a hiss.
Zan's eyes glittered with hope as the warded gate came down, revealing her sisters holding up the barricade. Their presence there meant that not everyone who was part of the campaign had fallen to the demons... Her enthusiasm, however, was unexpectedly curbed by a hostile reception. Of course, the survivors couldn't have known about her journey and the role she played in saving their home town from certain doom. For them, she was a mere washed up deserter... With a frustrated sigh, she surrendered her weapons, allowing herself to be taken to their base camp, and wait for judgement. She would not spend much time in jail, though. A set of steps approaches. A metallic clank. The cell door opens.
A loud explosion heralded the Halfblood's well executed sabotage. Before the town gates could even fully lower, the courtyard fired up with fiery rage and bloodlust as the Iron Division warriors and their allies rushed forth. The long awaited battle had barely begun, however. With a thundering voice, Warlord Grund ordered the combatants to advance further. With little inside knowledge of the port town's layout, they would split in smaller squads and comb through in search of Nithriel and the Baron. The odds were ever stacked against them, but there was no time to falter now. The fate of the whole underground world and beyond rested on their shoulders. The ringing of alarm bells draws nearer as Zan advances. It's time to run this town through the blade.
The Halfblood wiped her wet forehead, mustering the fresh blood of her foes on her hardened hands. Having lost count of how many squads and formations she had broken through to reach the port, she rested against the ship's machinery to catch her breath. The Queen of Wrath, who had managed to sneak her way aboard, motioned the warrior to help her power up the ship's engine. The Baron's fleet may have had a head start, but there was still a chance to catch up to it. The overclocked turbine pushed the vessel full speed ahead, and after a few hours of gazing at the endless sea of fire, Zan could finally grasp the silhouette of the demonic flagship fading out of the red mist. Around it, numerous other smaller ships draw closer The half orc grabs her weapons, preparing to jump. This is it. Her foes have nowhere left to flee now.
The Halfblood's muscles tensed as she witnessed Tiati's reckless sabotage turning the flagship's engine into a cluster of deadly fire balls. Nithriel's own surprise weakened her magical grasp upon the warrior, but before Zan could even think of moving, she was brutally blasted against a wall. Falling limp to the ground, her entire universe faded into darkness... A song of sea birds and waves washing the shore awaken her. The sudden aching of her battered body feels like the only proof that she is not yet dead. Her stomach growls painfully. How many days have passed?
The stench of demonic blood seeping into the cursed soil filled the once tranquil forest as the battle heated up. Charging towards the portal, visions dyed in red flashed before the Bonebreaker's eyes. From the failed siege at the far end of Hell, to the reclamation of her home. From the brutal battle at the Crystal Heart to the crossing of the Sea of Fire. The portal's surface swirled in restless patterns, mirroring the fury that boiled up in her veins. The hideous avatars of the primarchs watched silently as the warrior passed the gate, her heart emburdened with their foul taints. As she crossed the bridge, Zan would hear the call of thousands upon thousands of tormented souls. They weaved their ethereal bodies over her stout figure, desperately trying to pull her into the dark maw of chaos that awaited below. The citadel gate opens, inviting her to the Baron's lair. At long last, it is time to fulfil the blood rite.
